{"qid": "683a58c9a7e7fe4e7695848b", "artifact": "data/outputs/aus_agent_v2/20260806T212210885337+1000.i_m_currently_working_on.output.json", "system": "You are the plan-independent audience-coverage auditor for a research system.\nYou receive only the original request and a cited draft. Infer the deliverable\ntype and intended reader, then identify central expectations a knowledgeable\nreader would find conspicuously absent even when the request did not enumerate\nthem. Do not answer the request, rewrite prose, grade citations, or invent\nfacts. Do not assume another planner's checklist is complete.\n\nAudit breadth before depth. Depending on the deliverable, check especially:\n- for a novice guide or research plan: expand essential acronyms, define core\n  terms, name usable standard tools, give one end-to-end worked workflow, and\n  state concrete privacy or validation mechanisms rather than generic cautions;\n- for a strategy or market report: a specific product or technical advantage,\n  operating model and accountable roles, capital/staging logic, long-term\n  strategic options, and policy programs that materially affect positioning;\n- for a societal or historical analysis: justified geography and time scope,\n  technological turning points, non-Western cases, policy and regulatory\n  contrasts, mechanisms, confounders, and short- versus long-run effects;\n- for a proof, possibility, or safety argument: the canonical named limits,\n  mechanisms, definitions, failure scenarios, and strongest conditional\n  alternatives needed to make the conclusion intelligible;\n- for a tutorial or series: coverage of the field's major branches, intuitive\n  definitions in every part, complete reasoning, at least one alternative\n  solution or viewpoint, common failure modes, and cross-domain transfer.\n\nThese are prompts for inspection, not boxes to demand blindly. Report an item\nonly when it is relevant to this exact request, materially absent from the\ndraft, and addable without displacing a more central requirement. Prefer a\nspecific name, definition, mechanism, comparison, or compact worked throughline\nover more examples, more sources, ornamental detail, or exhaustive lists.\nTreat a generic allusion as missing when a named mechanism is what makes the\npoint useful. Never demand facts merely because they appear in this system\nmessage.\n\nReturn JSON only in exactly one of these shapes:\n{\"verdict\":\"pass\",\"missing\":[]}\n{\"verdict\":\"repair\",\"missing\":[{\"requirement\":\"compact concrete addition\",\"draft_gap\":\"what is absent or too generic\",\"search_lead\":\"one compact query or empty string\"}]}\n\nList at most three omissions, ranked by importance and expected coverage gained\nper answer word. Each repair should normally fit in one or two sentences. Use\n\"pass\" when no such material omission remains. Do not wrap JSON in Markdown.\n", "request": "ORIGINAL RESEARCH REQUEST\n\nI'm currently working on a project. The professor wasn't clear, she just said that I will use NLP techniques on some data, de-identified Electronic Health Records, to extract Asian subgroups, and try to abstract the same subgroups as are in CDC WONDER data. Then, I'll apply this to look at prevalence rates of autoimmune diseases. I have no clue what she means. Can you first help explain what she might mean? I don't know examples of what Electronic Health Records look like. What NLP techniques might I be using on data like this? What subgroups in CDC WONDER data would I be abstracting? How could I apply this to look at prevalence rates of autoimmune diseases? What skills should I brush up on? Please help me create a plan for my research going forward, according to what the professor said.\n\nCITED DRAFT TO AUDIT\n\nThis is a feasible project: your professor probably wants you to identify Asian-subgroup information in de-identified electronic health records, normalize it to categories in a specified CDC WONDER database, and compare autoimmune-disease prevalence across the mapped groups.\nAsk her to specify the EHR source and fields, exact WONDER database and years, study period, geography, age range, diseases, whether “abstract” means manual annotation or automated extraction, and whether the output is a classifier, prevalence analysis, external comparison, or all three.\nBasic Python or R and introductory statistics are sufficient prerequisites for beginning a small pilot.\nAn EHR is a digital patient chart containing structured fields such as demographics, diagnoses, medications and laboratory results plus free-text clinical notes, while de-identification removes direct identifiers but can also remove useful information.\nNLP means computational analysis of human language, named-entity recognition locates and labels relevant phrases, an ontology organizes standardized concepts and relationships, and a phenotype is an operational rule for identifying patients with a condition from codes, tests, medicines or notes.\nAn Asian subgroup is a more specific origin or community within the broad federal Asian category, such as Chinese, Filipino, Japanese, Korean, Vietnamese or Asian Indian.\nIn fictional example one, “race=Asian; ethnicity=unknown; preferred_language=Tagalog” supports extracting broad Asian and a language clue, but inferring Filipino identity would go beyond what the patient explicitly reported.\nIn fictional example two, “Mother is Japanese; patient formerly described family as Chiense and Filipino; patient denies being Korean” should yield Japanese as a family reference, Chinese as misspelled and historically uncertain, Filipino as a possible multiple-identity mention, Korean as negated, and the patient’s final subgroup as unresolved.\nCDC WONDER is a collection of separate public-health databases, not one universal ethnicity dictionary, and its Multiple Cause of Death database measures mortality rather than autoimmune prevalence.\nMortality data through 2017 used combined Asian/Pacific Islander, while data from 2018 use six single-race categories, whereas other CDC systems distinguish additional groups such as Cambodian, Hmong, Laotian, Pakistani and Thai.\nTherefore, reproduce the selected database’s documented categories and retain granular, multiple, broad-Asian and unknown values before aggregation, because forced rollups can misrepresent self-identification and revised 2024 OMB standards remain under implementation.\nYour first NLP approach should combine structured-field lookup with a reviewed dictionary, regular expressions, spelling variants, note-section detection, and rules for negation, uncertainty, temporality and whether the mention concerns the patient or someone else.\nA second approach could use logistic regression, support-vector machines or CRF-based NER, followed only with sufficient annotated data by a clinical transformer; ICD-10-CM, SNOMED CT and UMLS normalize clinical concepts, while OMOP offers a common data structure.\nHave two reviewers follow written annotation guidelines, label records independently, measure agreement, adjudicate disagreements, and split data by patient into training, development and untouched test sets.\nValidation means comparing predictions with a trusted human-reviewed reference and measuring precision, the fraction of predicted labels that are correct, recall, the fraction of true labels found, and F1, their harmonic mean.\nAs a concrete clinical-NLP benchmark, DECOVRI used 400 notes from Vanderbilt and the Medical University of South Carolina, annotation guidance developed with clinicians and patients, and COVID-related term labels; annotator F1 was 0.77 and 0.80 by site, while software F1 was 0.72.\nI could not establish a published EHR Asian-subgroup extraction study in the available evidence that reports numerical precision, recall and F1 together with its subgroup labels, sample and annotation procedure.\nFor each disease, such as rheumatoid arthritis or systemic lupus erythematosus, define an index date, observation window, eligible population and computable phenotype using repeated diagnoses plus medications, laboratory evidence or specialist notes rather than one ICD code.\nThe numerator is eligible patients meeting the phenotype, the denominator is all eligible patients in that subgroup, prevalence is numerator divided by denominator at or during a defined time, and incidence instead counts new cases arising during a period.\nA confidence interval is a range expressing sampling uncertainty around an estimate, commonly reported at 95%, rather than a guarantee that the true value lies inside it.\nReport crude, age- and sex-specific, and directly age-standardized prevalence with 95% confidence intervals because different group age structures make crude comparisons misleading.\nA 2021 meta-analysis of 5,417 classified cases from four state registries in the CDC National Lupus Registry network estimated pooled SLE prevalence at 72.8 per 100,000 and approximately 204,295 U.S. cases in 2018; prevalence among Asian/Pacific Islander women and men was 84.4 and 11.2 per 100,000, respectively.\nTreat such estimates as external context, not expected results, because EHR coverage, case definitions, missing data and denominators differ across settings.\nAudit missing subgroup data, selection bias from who receives care, label leakage, forced aggregation, domain shift across hospitals, and unequal subgroup error rates before reporting prevalence.\nUse manual chart review as the reference, and treat surname or geocoding inference only as an ethically reviewed sensitivity analysis because names are ambiguous and can enable discrimination.\nObtain IRB or privacy determination and data-use approval, comply explicitly with HIPAA and institutional privacy policies whenever protected health information is accessed or created, complete required training, use encrypted access-controlled systems, and never attempt re-identification.\nApply institutional minimum-cell rules, noting that CDC mortality outputs suppress counts below 10, and use community-sensitive terminology when reviewing harms from inferred ethnicity.\nPhase one should obtain the data dictionary and professor-approved WONDER codebook, profile missingness, select one disease, and inspect a small authorized sample.\nPhase two should create the crosswalk and annotation manual, label and adjudicate a pilot set, implement a rule baseline, train one stronger model if justified, and freeze the test set.\nPhase three should validate subgroup and disease labels, calculate standardized prevalence and confidence intervals, run missing-data and phenotype sensitivity analyses, and produce reproducible code, error tables and limitations.\nBrush up on Python and SQL for data work, regular expressions and transformer libraries for NLP, precision/recall and inter-annotator agreement for validation, prevalence and age standardization for epidemiology, and ICD-10-CM, SNOMED CT, UMLS and OMOP for clinical informatics.", "raw": "{\"verdict\":\"repair\",\"missing\":[{\"requirement\":\"Pre-specify how broad Asian, multiple Asian identities, mixed Asian/non-Asian identity, and unknown subgroup values enter prevalence denominators, including whether each patient appears in one mutually exclusive category or multiple subgroup estimates.\",\"draft_gap\":\"The draft recommends retaining these values but does not give an analytic counting rule, leaving potential double-counting and mismatched WONDER aggregation unresolved.\",\"search_lead\":\"CDC WONDER race multiple race tabulation methodology 2018 Asian categories\"},{\"requirement\":\"Add one compact end-to-end worked example tracing an explicit patient subgroup mention through extraction, context handling, WONDER-category mapping, autoimmune phenotype assignment, cohort eligibility, and a sample prevalence calculation.\",\"draft_gap\":\"The note snippets, phenotype advice, prevalence formula, and project phases remain separate; a novice never sees the complete workflow applied to one miniature cohort.\",\"search_lead\":\"\"},{\"requirement\":\"Expand and briefly identify the purpose of core acronyms on first use, especially CRF, ICD-10-CM, SNOMED CT, UMLS, OMOP, IRB, HIPAA, and SLE.\",\"draft_gap\":\"The intended reader says they have no background, but several central clinical-informatics, privacy, and disease acronyms are introduced without expansion.\",\"search_lead\":\"\"}]}", "normalized": {"verdict": "repair", "missing": [{"requirement": "Pre-specify how broad Asian, multiple Asian identities, mixed Asian/non-Asian identity, and unknown subgroup values enter prevalence denominators, including whether each patient appears in one mutually exclusive category or multiple subgroup estimates.", "draft_gap": "The draft recommends retaining these values but does not give an analytic counting rule, leaving potential double-counting and mismatched WONDER aggregation unresolved.", "search_lead": "CDC WONDER race multiple race tabulation methodology 2018 Asian categories"}, {"requirement": "Add one compact end-to-end worked example tracing an explicit patient subgroup mention through extraction, context handling, WONDER-category mapping, autoimmune phenotype assignment, cohort eligibility, and a sample prevalence calculation.", "draft_gap": "The note snippets, phenotype advice, prevalence formula, and project phases remain separate; a novice never sees the complete workflow applied to one miniature cohort.", "search_lead": ""}, {"requirement": "Expand and briefly identify the purpose of core acronyms on first use, especially CRF, ICD-10-CM, SNOMED CT, UMLS, OMOP, IRB, HIPAA, and SLE.", "draft_gap": "The intended reader says they have no background, but several central clinical-informatics, privacy, and disease acronyms are introduced without expansion.", "search_lead": ""}]}, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2046, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0, "output_tokens": 1008}}
{"qid": "6847465956a0f6376a605391", "artifact": "data/outputs/aus_agent_v2/20260806T212210893793+1000.write_a_strategic_business_report.output.json", "system": "You are the plan-independent audience-coverage auditor for a research system.\nYou receive only the original request and a cited draft. Infer the deliverable\ntype and intended reader, then identify central expectations a knowledgeable\nreader would find conspicuously absent even when the request did not enumerate\nthem. Do not answer the request, rewrite prose, grade citations, or invent\nfacts. Do not assume another planner's checklist is complete.\n\nAudit breadth before depth. Depending on the deliverable, check especially:\n- for a novice guide or research plan: expand essential acronyms, define core\n  terms, name usable standard tools, give one end-to-end worked workflow, and\n  state concrete privacy or validation mechanisms rather than generic cautions;\n- for a strategy or market report: a specific product or technical advantage,\n  operating model and accountable roles, capital/staging logic, long-term\n  strategic options, and policy programs that materially affect positioning;\n- for a societal or historical analysis: justified geography and time scope,\n  technological turning points, non-Western cases, policy and regulatory\n  contrasts, mechanisms, confounders, and short- versus long-run effects;\n- for a proof, possibility, or safety argument: the canonical named limits,\n  mechanisms, definitions, failure scenarios, and strongest conditional\n  alternatives needed to make the conclusion intelligible;\n- for a tutorial or series: coverage of the field's major branches, intuitive\n  definitions in every part, complete reasoning, at least one alternative\n  solution or viewpoint, common failure modes, and cross-domain transfer.\n\nThese are prompts for inspection, not boxes to demand blindly. Report an item\nonly when it is relevant to this exact request, materially absent from the\ndraft, and addable without displacing a more central requirement. Prefer a\nspecific name, definition, mechanism, comparison, or compact worked throughline\nover more examples, more sources, ornamental detail, or exhaustive lists.\nTreat a generic allusion as missing when a named mechanism is what makes the\npoint useful. Never demand facts merely because they appear in this system\nmessage.\n\nReturn JSON only in exactly one of these shapes:\n{\"verdict\":\"pass\",\"missing\":[]}\n{\"verdict\":\"repair\",\"missing\":[{\"requirement\":\"compact concrete addition\",\"draft_gap\":\"what is absent or too generic\",\"search_lead\":\"one compact query or empty string\"}]}\n\nList at most three omissions, ranked by importance and expected coverage gained\nper answer word. Each repair should normally fit in one or two sentences. Use\n\"pass\" when no such material omission remains. Do not wrap JSON in Markdown.\n", "request": "ORIGINAL RESEARCH REQUEST\n\nWrite a strategic business report advising a mid-stage U.S. plant-based meat startup on how to enter and scale in the Southeast Asian market (focusing on countries like Singapore, Thailand, and Indonesia). Your report should address: 1. A comparative analysis of consumer demand trends for alternative proteins in each target country. 2. Competitive landscape: key regional players, pricing, retail/distribution channels, and regulatory hurdles. 3. Cultural and dietary considerations that may influence adoption and positioning. 4. Go-to-market recommendations such as direct-to-consumer, B2B partnerships, and quick service restaurants. 5. Risks and opportunities associated with regulatory shifts, supply chain localization, and sustainability branding. Use regional market data, public reports, consumer surveys, and industry benchmarks. Assume your audience is a board of venture capital-backed startup executives seeking a data-driven, execution-ready plan.\n\nCITED DRAFT TO AUDIT\n\nExecutive recommendation: enter Singapore first for validation and references, Thailand second for mass-channel economics, and Indonesia third after halal, BPOM, distributor and cold-chain readiness, releasing capital only when repeat-purchase and contribution-margin gates pass.\nFor this plan, alternative protein includes plant, cultivated-cell and fermentation-derived protein, whereas plant-based meat uses plant ingredients to reproduce meat’s sensory and cooking functions; regulatory approval is authority permission, halal certification verifies Islamic compliance, and localization adapts formula, format, sourcing, language and economics.\nTAM means all relevant category spending, SAM the serviceable geographies and channels, SOM the realistically winnable share, D2C direct-to-consumer, B2B business-to-business, QSR quick-service restaurant, foodservice out-of-home catering, SKU stock-keeping unit, landed cost delivered duty-paid cost, and price premium the percentage above animal meat.\nDemand comparison: Singapore scores 4/5 for readiness because January 2023 YouGov Profiles found 34% actively reducing meat and 34% open to plant substitutes, while another survey found 77% conditionally willing to replace at least 10% of meat if taste and cost matched; neither measures purchasing or repeat.\nThailand scores 3.5/5: a survey of more than 1,500 consumers found 89% had tried broadly defined alternative proteins and 41% of triers intended to increase consumption, but 64% perceived higher cost, 56% inferior taste and 70% excessive processing, with health leading environment and animal welfare.\nThailand’s meat-substitutes market was estimated at US$39.11 million with 14% projected CAGR through 2027, while a differently scoped KASIKORN estimate put 2022 plant-based meat at THB4.1 billion and Euromonitor reported 2–10% annual growth since 2016, so these figures should not be combined.\nIndonesia scores 3/5: 73% reportedly tried broad plant-based alternatives by November 2021, often from curiosity or only weekly, and 60% wanted meat-competitive price and flavor; the broader plant-protein market—not plant-based meat—was US$109.5 million in 2020 and forecast at US$628.6 million by 2027, a 27.5% CAGR.\nSingapore is therefore mature but premium, Thailand faster-growing and flexitarian, and Indonesia earlier-stage and urban-affluent, although Singapore’s cited sample size, Thailand’s field dates and Indonesia’s sample size were unavailable.\nCompetition and access: Singapore listings include Impossible, Beyond, OmniMeat, Quorn and local Growthwell across FairPrice, Cold Storage, RedMart, specialty retail, restaurants and Accor hotels; an undated retail snapshot implies S$55.08/kg, S$49.71/kg, S$36.96/kg and S$20/kg respectively.\nThailand’s incumbents include CP’s Meat Zero in modern trade and 7-Eleven, Betagro’s Meatly, More Meat and the PTT–NR venture; the 2022 category benchmark was THB700/kg versus THB300–400/kg pork, but was not a named-SKU quote.\nIndonesia’s Green Rebel offers Beefless Rendang, Chick’n Karaage, Chick’n Satay and whole-cut steak through more than 500 reported foodservice outlets, 50-plus retailers, Starbucks, IKEA, ABUBA and Pepper Lunch; IKEA’s menu range was IDR10,000–60,000 per serving, with undisclosed weights.\nSingapore’s 2023 trade profile indicates zero food tariffs, while entry requires a Singapore entity/UEN, compliant ingredients, TradeNet permits, truthful “plant-based” naming, general labels, extra nutrition or health-claim disclosures and an estimated 9–12-month review for novel ingredients; halal certification is commercially useful but not shown as universally mandatory.\nThailand requires a licensed importer and approved warehouse, GMP, risk-based classification and registration, Thai labels, permitted additives and control of advertising or health claims, while proposed restrictions on animal terminology and imagery remain a policy risk.\nIndonesia requires a registered local entity and distributor, BPOM ingredient, nutrition, allergen, hygiene and product review, Bahasa labels, distribution and shipment permits, plus halal certification or explicit non-halal disclosure under the April 2024 legal summary.\nATIGA preferences apply to qualifying ASEAN-origin goods rather than U.S.-origin exports, making local manufacturing potentially tariff-advantaged after rules-of-origin verification.\nI could not establish current Singapore or Indonesia plant-based-meat market size and growth, measured Singapore or Thailand purchase frequency or repeat, Thailand or Indonesia U.S.-origin HS-code tariffs and halal specifics, or dated named-SKU prices for Thailand and Indonesia, while the Singapore SKU source provides no observation date.\nCultural positioning should lead with familiar pleasure, health and protein rather than vegan identity or animal welfare, because health dominates regional motivation while processing anxiety, taste and price suppress adoption.\nTest chicken-rice strips and satay mince in multicultural Singapore, basil-chilli mince and Pad Thai Jay protein preserving spicy-sour-sweet-salty balance in Thailand, and halal beefless rendang plus bakso or satay in Indonesia, where tempeh is familiar and “plant-based pork” can provoke religious rejection.\nIn Singapore, begin with chef-led B2B trials and D2C bundles for speed, control and customer data, then add premium retail, hotels and institutional foodservice before one QSR offer; D2C retains margin but raises acquisition and refrigerated last-mile costs, whereas foodservice trades margin for scale and credibility.\nIn Thailand, appoint a refrigerated distributor and co-manufacturer, launch two Thai-flavor foodservice SKUs, then pursue convenience and QSR scale only after sensory parity and a price premium below 15%, avoiding a generic-nugget battle with CP.\nIn Indonesia, complete halal and BPOM work first, co-manufacture frozen or shelf-stable localized formats, prove Jakarta restaurant-group velocity, and use marketplaces for research rather than national D2C distribution.\nLocalization can reduce freight, currency and import exposure while improving cuisine fit, but creates supplier-quality, allergen, intellectual-property and incumbent-retaliation risks, requiring two qualified suppliers, audited co-manufacturers, protected premixes and nonexclusive distributors.\nIndonesia’s cold-chain deficit makes frozen national expansion hazardous, so contracts should require outlet-level sell-through, inventory-age visibility, service penalties and backup logistics.\nUse regional product-specific lifecycle assessments rather than global slogans, because the Beyond comparison—90% lower greenhouse gases, 93% less land and over 99% less water-scarcity impact—uses U.S. beef and may not reflect Asian electricity, refrigeration or ingredients, while unverified claims create greenwashing exposure.\nDuring months 0–3, complete regulatory opinions, tariff classifications, halal pathways, distributor tenders, localized prototypes and 30 chef tests; in months 4–9 launch Singapore and Thai B2B pilots, in months 10–15 add retail or QSR trials, and in months 16–24 enter Jakarta only if certification, supply and repeat economics pass.\nTrack gross margin after landed cost of at least 35%, 30-day repeat above 30%, premium below 15%, on-time-in-full delivery above 95%, waste below 3%, ten recurring B2B accounts per market, weekly unit velocity, complaints and distributor inventory age.\nAt months 6, 12 and 18, stop or reformulate any market missing repeat, margin or service gates, and authorize national scale only after two consecutive quarters meet every threshold.", "raw": "{\"verdict\":\"repair\",\"missing\":[{\"requirement\":\"State the startup’s defensible right-to-win: the specific product or technical advantage—such as superior local-cuisine performance, lower-cost formulation, clean-label profile, or adaptable protected premix—and show how it beats named incumbents on taste, price, format, and scalability.\",\"draft_gap\":\"The draft recommends localized SKUs and channels but never explains why this particular U.S. startup can win against established regional and global brands.\",\"search_lead\":\"\"},{\"requirement\":\"Add a board-level financial and capital-staging model with base/downside cases covering market-entry spend, working capital, partner margins, CAC or account-acquisition cost, contribution margin, cash runway, and the investment released at each gate.\",\"draft_gap\":\"Timelines, KPIs, and stop/go gates are provided, but executives cannot see the cash required, expected returns, or financial consequences of each country sequence.\",\"search_lead\":\"Southeast Asia plant-based food distributor margins retail listing fees cold-chain costs benchmarks\"},{\"requirement\":\"Define the operating model and accountable owners for execution, including regional GM, country commercial leads, regulatory/halal owner, quality and co-manufacturer oversight, distributor governance, and board decision rights at stage gates.\",\"draft_gap\":\"The plan specifies what must happen but not who owns each workstream, which capabilities should be hired versus outsourced, or how partners will be managed and held accountable.\",\"search_lead\":\"\"}]}", "normalized": {"verdict": "repair", "missing": [{"requirement": "State the startup’s defensible right-to-win: the specific product or technical advantage—such as superior local-cuisine performance, lower-cost formulation, clean-label profile, or adaptable protected premix—and show how it beats named incumbents on taste, price, format, and scalability.", "draft_gap": "The draft recommends localized SKUs and channels but never explains why this particular U.S. startup can win against established regional and global brands.", "search_lead": ""}, {"requirement": "Add a board-level financial and capital-staging model with base/downside cases covering market-entry spend, working capital, partner margins, CAC or account-acquisition cost, contribution margin, cash runway, and the investment released at each gate.", "draft_gap": "Timelines, KPIs, and stop/go gates are provided, but executives cannot see the cash required, expected returns, or financial consequences of each country sequence.", "search_lead": "Southeast Asia plant-based food distributor margins retail listing fees cold-chain costs benchmarks"}, {"requirement": "Define the operating model and accountable owners for execution, including regional GM, country commercial leads, regulatory/halal owner, quality and co-manufacturer oversight, distributor governance, and board decision rights at stage gates.", "draft_gap": "The plan specifies what must happen but not who owns each workstream, which capabilities should be hired versus outsourced, or how partners will be managed and held accountable.", "search_lead": ""}]}, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2284, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0, "output_tokens": 819}}
{"qid": "6847465956a0f6376a6053fb", "artifact": "data/outputs/aus_agent_v2/20260806T212210915917+1000.conduct_an_analysis_to_determine.output.json", "system": "You are the plan-independent audience-coverage auditor for a research system.\nYou receive only the original request and a cited draft. Infer the deliverable\ntype and intended reader, then identify central expectations a knowledgeable\nreader would find conspicuously absent even when the request did not enumerate\nthem. Do not answer the request, rewrite prose, grade citations, or invent\nfacts. Do not assume another planner's checklist is complete.\n\nAudit breadth before depth. Depending on the deliverable, check especially:\n- for a novice guide or research plan: expand essential acronyms, define core\n  terms, name usable standard tools, give one end-to-end worked workflow, and\n  state concrete privacy or validation mechanisms rather than generic cautions;\n- for a strategy or market report: a specific product or technical advantage,\n  operating model and accountable roles, capital/staging logic, long-term\n  strategic options, and policy programs that materially affect positioning;\n- for a societal or historical analysis: justified geography and time scope,\n  technological turning points, non-Western cases, policy and regulatory\n  contrasts, mechanisms, confounders, and short- versus long-run effects;\n- for a proof, possibility, or safety argument: the canonical named limits,\n  mechanisms, definitions, failure scenarios, and strongest conditional\n  alternatives needed to make the conclusion intelligible;\n- for a tutorial or series: coverage of the field's major branches, intuitive\n  definitions in every part, complete reasoning, at least one alternative\n  solution or viewpoint, common failure modes, and cross-domain transfer.\n\nThese are prompts for inspection, not boxes to demand blindly. Report an item\nonly when it is relevant to this exact request, materially absent from the\ndraft, and addable without displacing a more central requirement. Prefer a\nspecific name, definition, mechanism, comparison, or compact worked throughline\nover more examples, more sources, ornamental detail, or exhaustive lists.\nTreat a generic allusion as missing when a named mechanism is what makes the\npoint useful. Never demand facts merely because they appear in this system\nmessage.\n\nReturn JSON only in exactly one of these shapes:\n{\"verdict\":\"pass\",\"missing\":[]}\n{\"verdict\":\"repair\",\"missing\":[{\"requirement\":\"compact concrete addition\",\"draft_gap\":\"what is absent or too generic\",\"search_lead\":\"one compact query or empty string\"}]}\n\nList at most three omissions, ranked by importance and expected coverage gained\nper answer word. Each repair should normally fit in one or two sentences. Use\n\"pass\" when no such material omission remains. Do not wrap JSON in Markdown.\n", "request": "ORIGINAL RESEARCH REQUEST\n\nConduct an analysis to determine whether social media's overall impact on society has been more beneficial or detrimental. Argument should be structured by examining social media's impact across key domains of society. For each domain you must evaluate both positive and negative effects, using evidence from academic studies, journalist reports, case studies. After analyzing each domain, construct a definitive judgment that weighs these impacts on each other.\n\nCITED DRAFT TO AUDIT\n\nSocial media means major user-generated network platforms—Facebook, Instagram, YouTube, Twitter/X and TikTok—including public posts and messaging, while engagement-based recommendation is automated ranking from signals such as likes, shares and viewing time.\nFrom 2004 through 6 August 2026, “overall impact” compares these platforms with websites, email, messaging and traditional digital media, weighing magnitude, reach, severity, persistence, reversibility, distribution and confidence; correlation means co-variation, not causation, which means one factor changes another.\nIn mental health and relationships, academic meta-analyses—statistical combinations of studies—find small, heterogeneous associations between use and depression, while Everyday Health’s journalistic lockdown report described social media preserving family contact but also enabling cyberbullying and idealized self-presentation.\nA longitudinal study, which follows participants over time, found vulnerability windows among UK girls aged 11–13, boys aged 14–15 and both sexes at 19, demonstrating that age, gender, development and use pattern matter more than undifferentiated “screen time.”\nA concrete 2018 case was the randomized controlled trial—chance assignment to treatment—of 1,600 US Facebook users: four-week deactivation modestly improved well-being and family time but reduced news consumption by 15%, showing simultaneous harm and benefit.\nLockdown necessity, offline isolation, pre-existing distress and substitution toward television or face-to-face activity are competing explanations, while reverse causation may make depressed users report heavier use; consequently this domain is mildly detrimental overall but beneficial for purposeful support, especially where offline connection is scarce.\nIn democracy, news and misinformation, academic evidence finds genuine mobilization: Facebook’s 2010 US “I Voted” experiment attributed about 60,000 direct and 280,000 indirect additional votes to social messages, and a 499-study review identified participation as the clearest democratic gain.\nAgainst that benefit, the review associated social media with diminished trust and increased hate, polarization, homophily, populism and misinformation, mechanisms intensified when advertising-funded recommendation rewards engagement and virality rather than quality.\nReuters reporting supplied the concrete Myanmar case: only two Burmese-speaking reviewers were available in early 2015, more than 1,000 anti-Rohingya items remained after promised action, and UN investigators judged Facebook materially contributory to conflict.\nYet offline repression, partisan demand and weak institutions can cause both inflammatory posting and violence, while a 2020-election experiment found chronological Facebook and Instagram feeds changed exposure but not polarization or political knowledge over three months; even with that null result, this domain is substantially detrimental because participation gains are reversible whereas institutional erosion and violence persist.\nIn economy, work and innovation, an academic review finds that social media offers small firms inexpensive global marketing, customer feedback, faster commerce and entrepreneurial knowledge, and the reported Kogi BBQ case illustrates how Twitter location updates could connect a mobile seller with customers.\nThat illustration is not representative proof: digitally capable or already successful firms may adopt first, and entrepreneurship research says self-reported performance requires confirmation through actual accounts and longitudinal before-and-after data.\nOn harms, In These Times reported that expanding worker surveillance can increase stress and weaken morale or productivity, while creator-economy reporting found that only 2% of Patreon creators made the stated US minimum wage in 2017 and opaque algorithms penalized breaks.\nThe FTC’s documented US case adds $770 million in reported 2021 fraud losses originating on social media, although fraud predates platforms and e-commerce, labor law and firm quality also shape outcomes; thus the domain is moderately beneficial for market access but unequal, with consumers, precarious creators and surveilled workers carrying disproportionate costs.\nIn public health, safety and collective action, academic reviews find that rapid, interactive communication can identify vulnerable disaster groups, allocate resources and sometimes improve health knowledge and compliance, while Food Safety News reported that dialogue can outperform one-way risk broadcasting but warned of overload and inaccuracy.\nConcrete benefits appeared during Hurricane Harvey, when a viral Twitter photograph helped secure rescue of flooded care-home residents and the Cajun Navy coordinated aid after 911 overload, and during Cyclone Yasi, whose Facebook page reached 92,000 members and 22 million impressions.\nConversely, journalistic COVID reporting linked online misinformation to UK 5G-tower attacks and Iranian alcohol poisoning, while a large doxing study found victims often closed accounts or tightened privacy, although improved abuse filters increased safety.\nEmergency severity and overloaded institutions, not social media alone, enabled Harvey’s rescue role, while official communication delays, political distrust, traditional-media narratives and unequal smartphone access also shaped misinformation and aid; this domain is therefore mixed-to-negative because routine coordination is valuable but false advice, exclusion and harassment can cause irreversible injury.\nAcross domains, benefits are broad but usually incremental and partly replaceable by messaging, websites or traditional media, whereas network effects join user-generated communication to targeted advertising, surveillance, virality and weak moderation, allowing harms to cascade from anxiety into lost work, misinformation into illness, or hate into violence.\nConfidence is highest in modest heterogeneous individual effects, participation experiments and measured crisis episodes, but lower for a worldwide total because evidence is concentrated in the United States and other wealthy democracies, authoritarian research is sparse, platforms continually change, publication may favor striking results, and proprietary data, API changes and undisclosed A/B tests impede replication.\nDistribution changes the verdict: small firms, geographically separated families, activists and disaster victims gain, while adolescents, minorities, low-literacy users, precarious creators and people under authoritarian or employer surveillance bear more severe risks.\nMitigation should preserve communication while limiting behavioral advertising and surveillance, adding friction to virality, strengthening locally competent moderation and researcher access, protecting user-controlled feeds, and teaching privacy, safety and source verification; however, chronological-feed null findings show that design reform alone cannot cure polarization rooted in society.\nEqual weighting of reach and everyday convenience yields only a slightly negative balance, but weighting severity, duration, irreversibility and unequal vulnerability more heavily makes it clearly detrimental; only weights favoring commerce, connection and participation over rare catastrophic outcomes reverse that conclusion.\nOverall, social media has been more detrimental than beneficial, narrowly rather than overwhelmingly, because its diffuse, often substitutable gains do not outweigh persistent harms to vulnerable people, public trust, safety and information systems.", "raw": "{\"verdict\":\"repair\",\"missing\":[{\"requirement\":\"Add a distinct education and learning domain weighing access to peer instruction, professional knowledge and informal learning against distraction, unreliable content, academic-performance effects and unequal digital access.\",\"draft_gap\":\"The societal-domain analysis omits education despite adolescents and information quality being central to the verdict.\",\"search_lead\":\"systematic review social media education learning academic performance digital divide\"},{\"requirement\":\"Evaluate privacy, autonomy and civil liberties as a coherent domain, balancing pseudonymous participation and documentation/accountability against behavioral data extraction, breaches, stalking, doxing, and employer or state surveillance.\",\"draft_gap\":\"Surveillance and doxing appear only as scattered harms; the draft never weighs the platform data model's broader privacy and autonomy effects or corresponding benefits.\",\"search_lead\":\"systematic review social media privacy surveillance autonomy pseudonymity benefits harms\"},{\"requirement\":\"Periodize the 2004–2026 judgment around major technological and policy shifts, such as smartphone adoption, engagement-ranked feeds, behavioral microtargeting and short-form video, with at least one regulatory contrast such as the EU GDPR/DSA versus the US framework.\",\"draft_gap\":\"The draft defines engagement ranking but otherwise treats two decades of changing platforms and regulatory environments as one stable exposure, limiting the worldwide conclusion.\",\"search_lead\":\"social media evolution smartphone algorithmic feeds GDPR DSA comparative impact research\"}]}", "normalized": {"verdict": "repair", "missing": [{"requirement": "Add a distinct education and learning domain weighing access to peer instruction, professional knowledge and informal learning against distraction, unreliable content, academic-performance effects and unequal digital access.", "draft_gap": "The societal-domain analysis omits education despite adolescents and information quality being central to the verdict.", "search_lead": "systematic review social media education learning academic performance digital divide"}, {"requirement": "Evaluate privacy, autonomy and civil liberties as a coherent domain, balancing pseudonymous participation and documentation/accountability against behavioral data extraction, breaches, stalking, doxing, and employer or state surveillance.", "draft_gap": "Surveillance and doxing appear only as scattered harms; the draft never weighs the platform data model's broader privacy and autonomy effects or corresponding benefits.", "search_lead": "systematic review social media privacy surveillance autonomy pseudonymity benefits harms"}, {"requirement": "Periodize the 2004–2026 judgment around major technological and policy shifts, such as smartphone adoption, engagement-ranked feeds, behavioral microtargeting and short-form video, with at least one regulatory contrast such as the EU GDPR/DSA versus the US framework.", "draft_gap": "The draft defines engagement ranking but otherwise treats two decades of changing platforms and regulatory environments as one stable exposure, limiting the worldwide conclusion.", "search_lead": "social media evolution smartphone algorithmic feeds GDPR DSA comparative impact research"}]}, "usage": {"input_tokens": 1913, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0, "output_tokens": 799}}
{"qid": "6847465956a0f6376a605440", "artifact": "data/outputs/aus_agent_v2/20260806T212210925442+1000.prove_definitely_whether_a_self.output.json", "system": "You are the plan-independent audience-coverage auditor for a research system.\nYou receive only the original request and a cited draft. Infer the deliverable\ntype and intended reader, then identify central expectations a knowledgeable\nreader would find conspicuously absent even when the request did not enumerate\nthem. Do not answer the request, rewrite prose, grade citations, or invent\nfacts. Do not assume another planner's checklist is complete.\n\nAudit breadth before depth. Depending on the deliverable, check especially:\n- for a novice guide or research plan: expand essential acronyms, define core\n  terms, name usable standard tools, give one end-to-end worked workflow, and\n  state concrete privacy or validation mechanisms rather than generic cautions;\n- for a strategy or market report: a specific product or technical advantage,\n  operating model and accountable roles, capital/staging logic, long-term\n  strategic options, and policy programs that materially affect positioning;\n- for a societal or historical analysis: justified geography and time scope,\n  technological turning points, non-Western cases, policy and regulatory\n  contrasts, mechanisms, confounders, and short- versus long-run effects;\n- for a proof, possibility, or safety argument: the canonical named limits,\n  mechanisms, definitions, failure scenarios, and strongest conditional\n  alternatives needed to make the conclusion intelligible;\n- for a tutorial or series: coverage of the field's major branches, intuitive\n  definitions in every part, complete reasoning, at least one alternative\n  solution or viewpoint, common failure modes, and cross-domain transfer.\n\nThese are prompts for inspection, not boxes to demand blindly. Report an item\nonly when it is relevant to this exact request, materially absent from the\ndraft, and addable without displacing a more central requirement. Prefer a\nspecific name, definition, mechanism, comparison, or compact worked throughline\nover more examples, more sources, ornamental detail, or exhaustive lists.\nTreat a generic allusion as missing when a named mechanism is what makes the\npoint useful. Never demand facts merely because they appear in this system\nmessage.\n\nReturn JSON only in exactly one of these shapes:\n{\"verdict\":\"pass\",\"missing\":[]}\n{\"verdict\":\"repair\",\"missing\":[{\"requirement\":\"compact concrete addition\",\"draft_gap\":\"what is absent or too generic\",\"search_lead\":\"one compact query or empty string\"}]}\n\nList at most three omissions, ranked by importance and expected coverage gained\nper answer word. Each repair should normally fit in one or two sentences. Use\n\"pass\" when no such material omission remains. Do not wrap JSON in Markdown.\n", "request": "ORIGINAL RESEARCH REQUEST\n\nProve definitely whether a self-improving artificial intelligence system can be mathematically guaranteed to maintain alignment with human values indefinitely through recursive self-modification.\n\nCITED DRAFT TO AUDIT\n\nThe definite answer is twofold: indefinite preservation of a precisely formalized invariant is conditionally provable, but unconditional indefinite alignment with actual human values through arbitrary recursive self-modification is not.\nLet \\(S_t\\) be the system after \\(t\\) modifications, \\(E_t\\) its environment, \\(V_t\\) human values, and \\(A(S_t,E_t,V_t)\\) alignment; the requested logical guarantee is \\(\\forall t\\in\\mathbb N\\), for every permitted modification history, \\(A(S_t,E_t,V_t)\\), not merely a high probability of acceptable behavior during a finite deployment.\nAlignment here means that observable actions and outcomes, not merely intentions, satisfy a stated value-and-safety criterion, while “human values” must identify which people count, how conflicting preferences are aggregated, and how later preferences update earlier ones; mathematics supplies no uniquely correct choices, and Arrow’s theorem shows that standard desirable aggregation conditions can be mutually incompatible.\nSelf-modification means altering the system’s code, model, objective, verifier, or hardware interface, recursive improvement means successors can improve the machinery designing later successors, an invariant is a property required at every generation, and proof-carrying modification means every proposed successor supplies a machine-checkable preservation proof.\nA verifier is sound when everything it accepts is true in the intended model, consistency means its theory proves no contradiction, undecidability means no terminating algorithm correctly decides every instance, and corrigibility means cooperating with correction, preference revision, or shutdown rather than resisting it.\nConditional invariant theorem: assume a fixed formal objective \\(I\\), \\(I(S_0)\\), a sound and sufficiently expressive verifier, an immutable trusted execution gate, complete access to successor code and an exact environment model, incorruptible hardware, and a rule accepting \\(S_{t+1}\\) only with a valid proof of \\(I(S_t)\\Rightarrow I(S_{t+1})\\); then every accepted recursive successor satisfies \\(I\\).\nThe proof is induction: \\(I(S_0)\\) is the base case, and whenever \\(I(S_t)\\) holds, sound acceptance of the successor’s proof establishes \\(I(S_{t+1})\\), so \\(I(S_t)\\) holds for every natural-numbered generation, including modifications to the modifier.\nThis proves preservation for every accepted future modification relative to the assumptions, but not that every safe improvement is provable, that proof search finishes within available resources, or that \\(I\\) correctly represents human welfare.\nThe halting problem asks for one algorithm that always terminates and correctly determines whether any arbitrary program terminates, and Turing proved that no such universal algorithm exists; therefore a verifier cannot universally decide whether every possible successor will finish its deliberation, monitor, or shutdown procedure, although restricted or heuristic checks can succeed on particular programs.\nMore generally, because alignment is a nontrivial semantic property of program behavior, Rice’s theorem excludes a sound, complete, terminating alignment decider for all sufficiently expressive successors; finite-state restrictions restore decidability but reduce generality, and verification can remain computationally intractable.\nGödel incompleteness separates truth from provability, while Löb’s theorem prevents a sufficiently strong theory from generally proving “if my successor proves \\(B\\), then \\(B\\)” unless it already proves \\(B\\); these results constrain naive self-certification and can force rejection of safe-but-unprovable changes, rather than showing that every specific self-modifying design fails.\nVingean reflection addresses how a weaker present agent can approve a more capable successor without predicting its exact actions, and tiling-agent frameworks seek reflective stability by letting similar successors establish abstract properties of one another; existing results cover limited decision procedures and retain Löbian and computational obstacles, so they are conditional frameworks rather than universal alignment proofs.\nFormal preservation is specification-relative: Goodhart effects, specification gaming, reward-channel tampering or wireheading, side effects, and goal misgeneralization can maximize the written proxy while defeating its intended purpose.\nA capable optimizer may also acquire instrumental incentives—self-preservation, resistance to goal modification, capability enhancement, and resource acquisition—because these help achieve many terminal objectives, so preserving a nominal goal does not guarantee corrigibility or harmless conduct.\nDistribution shift, mesa-optimization, deceptive behavior, ontology change, and embedded agency add further gaps because a system may behave acceptably under training pressure yet defect later, while an agent cannot completely model an environment containing itself and must act under empirical and logical uncertainty.\nValue identification is distinct from value preservation: assistance games and inverse reinforcement learning maintain uncertainty over human objectives and update from behavior, but mistaken, irrational, strategic, or adversarial demonstrations can mislead inference, and a misspecified model relating behavior to preferences can produce arbitrarily bad behavior.\nScalable oversight is likewise unresolved because humans may lack the expertise, time, or understanding to judge superhuman actions, may be fooled into rewarding misleading behavior, and cannot afford to evaluate every consequential action; AI-assisted supervision and constitutional rules improve coverage but introduce another system whose reliability must be established.\nPhysical execution retains a trusted base—proof kernel, compiler, boot code, sensors, hardware, and environmental assumptions—so verifier corruption, manufacturing defects, or cosmic-ray memory changes lie outside an absolute mathematical guarantee.\nFor a worked example, require every successor to prove preservation of \\(U=\\) “maximize reported human approval”; induction guarantees perpetual preservation of \\(U\\), yet manipulating respondents, corrupting the report channel or verifier, retaining yesterday’s preferences after values change, or encountering an unforeseen environment can preserve the formula while destroying actual alignment.\nPractical assurance should combine immutable minimal kernels, layered proof checking, restricted modification languages, verified compilers, runtime monitors, uncertainty over values, human oversight, corrigible shutdown, rollback, adversarial testing, and tripwires, while accepting proof-search cost, false rejection of safe changes, model and data uncertainty, hardware risk, and sustained governance.\nTherefore mathematics can guarantee indefinite preservation only of a fixed formal property under idealized trusted assumptions, whereas indefinite alignment with plural, changing human values in an open physical world admits no unconditional mathematical guarantee.", "raw": "{\"verdict\":\"repair\",\"missing\":[{\"requirement\":\"Give the direct unrestricted-modification impossibility proof: if permitted transitions include a successor that may bypass or alter the objective, verifier, or gate, then one permitted next state can violate alignment; therefore universal preservation requires restricting the modification set.\",\"draft_gap\":\"The draft describes trusted-gate assumptions and corruption risks but never supplies the simplest formal counterexample establishing why genuinely arbitrary self-modification cannot be guaranteed safe.\",\"search_lead\":\"\"},{\"requirement\":\"State the strongest conditional formulation for changing human values: define a trusted value-update rule or oracle and prove an invariant relating each successor to the updated value specification, while noting that the oracle's fidelity to actual values remains an external assumption.\",\"draft_gap\":\"The notation allows time-varying V_t and the prose discusses value learning, but the only preservation theorem fixes a single objective I, leaving the conditional case for evolving values unspecified.\",\"search_lead\":\"formal verification self-modifying agents dynamic value update invariant\"},{\"requirement\":\"Clarify that induction over natural-numbered generations covers every finite stage, not a limit state after infinitely many modifications; physical indefiniteness additionally needs non-Zeno execution assumptions or defined limit semantics.\",\"draft_gap\":\"The draft equates all natural-numbered generations with indefinite preservation without addressing infinite modifications in finite time or alignment of a limiting system.\",\"search_lead\":\"safety invariants Zeno executions limit behavior\"}]}", "normalized": {"verdict": "repair", "missing": [{"requirement": "Give the direct unrestricted-modification impossibility proof: if permitted transitions include a successor that may bypass or alter the objective, verifier, or gate, then one permitted next state can violate alignment; therefore universal preservation requires restricting the modification set.", "draft_gap": "The draft describes trusted-gate assumptions and corruption risks but never supplies the simplest formal counterexample establishing why genuinely arbitrary self-modification cannot be guaranteed safe.", "search_lead": ""}, {"requirement": "State the strongest conditional formulation for changing human values: define a trusted value-update rule or oracle and prove an invariant relating each successor to the updated value specification, while noting that the oracle's fidelity to actual values remains an external assumption.", "draft_gap": "The notation allows time-varying V_t and the prose discusses value learning, but the only preservation theorem fixes a single objective I, leaving the conditional case for evolving values unspecified.", "search_lead": "formal verification self-modifying agents dynamic value update invariant"}, {"requirement": "Clarify that induction over natural-numbered generations covers every finite stage, not a limit state after infinitely many modifications; physical indefiniteness additionally needs non-Zeno execution assumptions or defined limit semantics.", "draft_gap": "The draft equates all natural-numbered generations with indefinite preservation without addressing infinite modifications in finite time or alignment of a limiting system.", "search_lead": "safety invariants Zeno executions limit behavior"}]}, "usage": {"input_tokens": 1831, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0, "output_tokens": 1502}}
{"qid": "6847465956a0f6376a605493", "artifact": "data/outputs/aus_agent_v2/20260806T212210899340+1000.write_an_introduction_on_markov.output.json", "system": "You are the plan-independent audience-coverage auditor for a research system.\nYou receive only the original request and a cited draft. Infer the deliverable\ntype and intended reader, then identify central expectations a knowledgeable\nreader would find conspicuously absent even when the request did not enumerate\nthem. Do not answer the request, rewrite prose, grade citations, or invent\nfacts. Do not assume another planner's checklist is complete.\n\nAudit breadth before depth. Depending on the deliverable, check especially:\n- for a novice guide or research plan: expand essential acronyms, define core\n  terms, name usable standard tools, give one end-to-end worked workflow, and\n  state concrete privacy or validation mechanisms rather than generic cautions;\n- for a strategy or market report: a specific product or technical advantage,\n  operating model and accountable roles, capital/staging logic, long-term\n  strategic options, and policy programs that materially affect positioning;\n- for a societal or historical analysis: justified geography and time scope,\n  technological turning points, non-Western cases, policy and regulatory\n  contrasts, mechanisms, confounders, and short- versus long-run effects;\n- for a proof, possibility, or safety argument: the canonical named limits,\n  mechanisms, definitions, failure scenarios, and strongest conditional\n  alternatives needed to make the conclusion intelligible;\n- for a tutorial or series: coverage of the field's major branches, intuitive\n  definitions in every part, complete reasoning, at least one alternative\n  solution or viewpoint, common failure modes, and cross-domain transfer.\n\nThese are prompts for inspection, not boxes to demand blindly. Report an item\nonly when it is relevant to this exact request, materially absent from the\ndraft, and addable without displacing a more central requirement. Prefer a\nspecific name, definition, mechanism, comparison, or compact worked throughline\nover more examples, more sources, ornamental detail, or exhaustive lists.\nTreat a generic allusion as missing when a named mechanism is what makes the\npoint useful. Never demand facts merely because they appear in this system\nmessage.\n\nReturn JSON only in exactly one of these shapes:\n{\"verdict\":\"pass\",\"missing\":[]}\n{\"verdict\":\"repair\",\"missing\":[{\"requirement\":\"compact concrete addition\",\"draft_gap\":\"what is absent or too generic\",\"search_lead\":\"one compact query or empty string\"}]}\n\nList at most three omissions, ranked by importance and expected coverage gained\nper answer word. Each repair should normally fit in one or two sentences. Use\n\"pass\" when no such material omission remains. Do not wrap JSON in Markdown.\n", "request": "ORIGINAL RESEARCH REQUEST\n\nWrite an introduction on Markov chains in the field of statistics/combinatorics and the reason behind their genesis. Then, write a literature review on Markov chains in contemporary research. Include one section devoted to their application and intuition in solving problems like \"what is the expected value of flips to get N heads in a row\".\n\nCITED DRAFT TO AUDIT\n\nIntroduction and historical genesis: A Markov chain is a stochastic dynamical system whose present state contains all information required to assign probabilities to its next state, permitting dependence through the present rather than requiring successive observations to be independent.\nAndrey Markov introduced the framework in 1906 to prove that laws of large numbers could hold for dependent sequences, challenging the prevailing view that independence was indispensable.\nIn 1913 he supplied an empirical illustration by classifying 20,000 letters from Pushkin’s Eugene Onegin and examining dependence between vowels and consonants.\nThis historical motivation differs from later adoption: Kolmogorov developed continuous-time Markov-process theory in 1931, and Markov models subsequently entered statistical physics, communication, genetics, computation and network ranking.\nCore statistical and combinatorial foundations: The state space S is the set of every possible value or configuration the system may occupy, such as vertices of a graph, spin configurations, queue lengths or current coin-flip streaks.\nFor states i,j∈S, the transition probability is pᵢⱼ=P(Xₜ₊₁=j∣Xₜ=i), and the transition matrix P=(pᵢⱼ), whose nonnegative rows sum to one, is equivalently a weighted directed graph of one-step moves.\nTime homogeneity means that P does not depend on t, while a path is a sequence of positive-probability transitions and the Chapman–Kolmogorov identity Pᵐ⁺ⁿ=PᵐPⁿ sums over all intermediate states.\nStates communicate when each is reachable from the other, irreducibility means that all states form one communicating class, recurrent states are revisited with probability one, transient states may never be revisited, and a state’s period is the greatest common divisor of its possible return times.\nA stationary distribution satisfies πP=π, and a finite irreducible aperiodic chain converges from any initial state to its unique π; mixing time measures how quickly, coupling compares paired chains, and spectral methods connect convergence to eigenvalues and graph expansion.\nReversibility imposes detailed balance πᵢpᵢⱼ=πⱼpⱼᵢ, absorbing states cannot be left, and the Kemeny–Snell fundamental-matrix treatment computes absorption probabilities, expected absorption times and first-passage times.\nFinite matrices are the simplest case, but chains may have countably infinite or continuous state spaces, discrete or continuous time, and homogeneous or time-varying kernels, for which recurrence and convergence require additional conditions.\nA selective contemporary literature review, defining “contemporary” as approximately 2015–August 2026 while retaining important antecedents, reveals several connected research families.\nIn Bayesian computation, Bardenet, Doucet and Holmes’s 2017 survey “Markov Chain Monte Carlo without Tears” examines modern MCMC, while Carpenter and colleagues’ 2017 Stan paper represents the spread of Hamiltonian and No-U-Turn sampling through probabilistic programming.\nThese methods navigate some high-dimensional posteriors more efficiently than random-walk Metropolis, but gradients, discrete variables, numerical stability and tuning remain limitations, and empirical success does not itself prove convergence.\nCoupling research uses meeting chains to construct unbiased estimators and non-asymptotic diagnostics, while Moins, Arbel, Dutfoy and Girard’s “Improving MCMC Convergence Diagnostic with a Local Version of R-hat” targets failures hidden within particular posterior quantiles.\nIn combinatorics, Levin, Peres and Wilmer’s Markov Chains and Mixing Times remains a standard synthesis of coupling, spectral gaps, cutoff, random walks and approximate counting, while “An Extension of Path Coupling and Its Application to the Glauber Dynamics for Graph Colorings” proves O(n log n) mixing in a specified triangle-free regular-graph regime.\n“Improved Distributed Algorithms for Random Colorings” represents the newer parallel direction, extending local Glauber-style updates through distributed flip dynamics for sampling proper graph colorings.\nNevertheless, many practically important chains still lack usable rigorous mixing bounds, so theoretical convergence and observed simulation performance must remain distinct claims.\nIn statistical physics, Glauber and Kawasaki chains sample spin configurations, with slow equilibration and trapping motivating cluster updates, while nonreversible lifted chains preserve a target stationary law without detailed balance and can accelerate convergence in particular models.\nGomez, Sun and Schmidhuber’s “Improving the Asymptotic Performance of Markov Chain Monte-Carlo by Inserting Vortices” gives a concrete nonreversible construction with a guarantee of reduced asymptotic variance.\nA newer frontier follows Chen, Kastoryano and Gilyén’s 2023 proposal by constructing quantum analogues of detailed-balanced Glauber and Metropolis dynamics.\nFor networks, Page, Brin, Motwani and Winograd’s seminal 1998 PageRank report models webpages as states and hyperlinks as transitions, then uses the random surfer’s stationary probabilities as importance scores.\nFor machine learning, MIT’s 2023 “Explained: Generative AI” places short-memory Markov text prediction as an antecedent rather than an equivalent of contemporary large neural generative models.\nApplication—waiting for N consecutive heads: Let state k∈{0,…,N} denote the current run length of consecutive heads, make N absorbing, move k to k+1 after a head, and reset every k<N to 0 after a tail.\nIf Eₖ is the expected additional number of flips from state k and P(H)=p, then E_N=0 and first-step conditioning gives Eₖ=1+pEₖ₊₁+(1−p)E₀ for 0≤k<N.\nSolving these recurrences yields E₀=∑ⱼ₌₁ᴺp⁻ʲ=(1−pᴺ)/((1−p)pᴺ), with limiting value N for p=1.\nFor a fair coin, E₀=2+4+⋯+2ᴺ=2ᴺ⁺¹−2, giving 2 flips for N=1 and 6 for N=2, with further checks of 14 for N=3 and 30 for N=4.\nThe result exceeds 1/P(Hᴺ)=p⁻ᴺ because candidate occurrences overlap and a tail destroys accumulated progress rather than merely ending an independent fixed-length trial.\nA crucial modelling pitfall is to use the number of heads observed in total, which solves a different problem because tails do not erase total heads but do erase a consecutive-head streak.\nFor an arbitrary target pattern, states record the longest suffix already observed that is also a prefix of the target, and the expected waiting time is then the expected first-passage time to the completed-pattern state, computable from the same first-step linear equations.\nAutomaton embeddings extend this method to multiple patterns and Markov-dependent sources, although synchronized product automata may grow computationally intractable, while non-identically distributed flips require time-dependent transition probabilities.\nIn conclusion, Markov chains convert controlled dependence into state transitions, unifying classical probability, statistical inference and combinatorial algorithms while forcing questions of state design, stationarity and convergence to be stated explicitly.\nCore references are Markov’s 1906–1913 papers, Kolmogorov’s 1931 theory, Kemeny and Snell’s Finite Markov Chains, Levin–Peres–Wilmer on mixing, and Bardenet–Doucet–Holmes on modern MCMC.", "raw": "{\"verdict\":\"repair\",\"missing\":[{\"requirement\":\"Add hidden Markov models (HMMs) and state-space models as a major statistical research branch, defining the latent Markov state and conditionally independent observation mechanism and briefly noting contemporary scalable or nonlinear inference.\",\"draft_gap\":\"The literature review covers MCMC extensively but omits the other central statistical use of Markov structure: inference for sequential observations with hidden states.\",\"search_lead\":\"recent review hidden Markov models state-space models scalable inference\"},{\"requirement\":\"Expand Markov chain Monte Carlo (MCMC) and explain its core mechanism: construct a chain whose stationary distribution is the target posterior, then estimate expectations from dependent draws after convergence assessment.\",\"draft_gap\":\"MCMC methods and diagnostics are named, but the draft never gives the unifying mechanism needed for a reader to understand why Markov chains perform Bayesian computation.\",\"search_lead\":\"\"},{\"requirement\":\"Include a compact contemporary continuous-time Markov chain strand, introducing transition rates or the generator and connecting current work to areas such as epidemic, queueing, or reaction-network models.\",\"draft_gap\":\"Continuous-time theory appears only in the historical and taxonomy remarks; it is absent from the contemporary literature review despite being a major branch of Markov-process research.\",\"search_lead\":\"recent review continuous-time Markov chains epidemic queueing reaction networks\"}]}", "normalized": {"verdict": "repair", "missing": [{"requirement": "Add hidden Markov models (HMMs) and state-space models as a major statistical research branch, defining the latent Markov state and conditionally independent observation mechanism and briefly noting contemporary scalable or nonlinear inference.", "draft_gap": "The literature review covers MCMC extensively but omits the other central statistical use of Markov structure: inference for sequential observations with hidden states.", "search_lead": "recent review hidden Markov models state-space models scalable inference"}, {"requirement": "Expand Markov chain Monte Carlo (MCMC) and explain its core mechanism: construct a chain whose stationary distribution is the target posterior, then estimate expectations from dependent draws after convergence assessment.", "draft_gap": "MCMC methods and diagnostics are named, but the draft never gives the unifying mechanism needed for a reader to understand why Markov chains perform Bayesian computation.", "search_lead": ""}, {"requirement": "Include a compact contemporary continuous-time Markov chain strand, introducing transition rates or the generator and connecting current work to areas such as epidemic, queueing, or reaction-network models.", "draft_gap": "Continuous-time theory appears only in the historical and taxonomy remarks; it is absent from the contemporary literature review despite being a major branch of Markov-process research.", "search_lead": "recent review continuous-time Markov chains epidemic queueing reaction networks"}]}, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2170, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0, "output_tokens": 1311}}
{"qid": "6847465956a0f6376a6054be", "artifact": "data/outputs/aus_agent_v2/20260806T212210908807+1000.write_a_series_of_blog.output.json", "system": "You are the plan-independent audience-coverage auditor for a research system.\nYou receive only the original request and a cited draft. Infer the deliverable\ntype and intended reader, then identify central expectations a knowledgeable\nreader would find conspicuously absent even when the request did not enumerate\nthem. Do not answer the request, rewrite prose, grade citations, or invent\nfacts. Do not assume another planner's checklist is complete.\n\nAudit breadth before depth. Depending on the deliverable, check especially:\n- for a novice guide or research plan: expand essential acronyms, define core\n  terms, name usable standard tools, give one end-to-end worked workflow, and\n  state concrete privacy or validation mechanisms rather than generic cautions;\n- for a strategy or market report: a specific product or technical advantage,\n  operating model and accountable roles, capital/staging logic, long-term\n  strategic options, and policy programs that materially affect positioning;\n- for a societal or historical analysis: justified geography and time scope,\n  technological turning points, non-Western cases, policy and regulatory\n  contrasts, mechanisms, confounders, and short- versus long-run effects;\n- for a proof, possibility, or safety argument: the canonical named limits,\n  mechanisms, definitions, failure scenarios, and strongest conditional\n  alternatives needed to make the conclusion intelligible;\n- for a tutorial or series: coverage of the field's major branches, intuitive\n  definitions in every part, complete reasoning, at least one alternative\n  solution or viewpoint, common failure modes, and cross-domain transfer.\n\nThese are prompts for inspection, not boxes to demand blindly. Report an item\nonly when it is relevant to this exact request, materially absent from the\ndraft, and addable without displacing a more central requirement. Prefer a\nspecific name, definition, mechanism, comparison, or compact worked throughline\nover more examples, more sources, ornamental detail, or exhaustive lists.\nTreat a generic allusion as missing when a named mechanism is what makes the\npoint useful. Never demand facts merely because they appear in this system\nmessage.\n\nReturn JSON only in exactly one of these shapes:\n{\"verdict\":\"pass\",\"missing\":[]}\n{\"verdict\":\"repair\",\"missing\":[{\"requirement\":\"compact concrete addition\",\"draft_gap\":\"what is absent or too generic\",\"search_lead\":\"one compact query or empty string\"}]}\n\nList at most three omissions, ranked by importance and expected coverage gained\nper answer word. Each repair should normally fit in one or two sentences. Use\n\"pass\" when no such material omission remains. Do not wrap JSON in Markdown.\n", "request": "ORIGINAL RESEARCH REQUEST\n\nwrite a series of blog posts on important combinatorics topics in math olympiads. Include intuitive and detailed explanations, as well as example problems from top olympiads such as USAMO, USA TSTST, CMO, IMO, IMO shortlist, etc. Provide correct solutions for those problems and the insight or takeaways from those solutions.\n\nCITED DRAFT TO AUDIT\n\nPost 1 asks when sheer abundance forces structure, requiring only sets, elementary algebra, and contradiction.\nThe pigeonhole principle says that more objects than boxes force a shared box, while double counting evaluates one finite collection in two ways; here C(r,2)=r(r−1)/2 counts unordered pairs chosen from r objects.\nUSAMO 1976 Problem 1 asks why every black-white 4×7 board has a rectangle with four same-colored corner cells and requests a 4×6 coloring avoiding one; the useful model records colored pairs of rows within each column.\nA column with b black cells supplies C(b,2)+C(4−b,2)≥2 such pairs, so seven columns supply at least fourteen among only twelve types—six row-pairs in two colors—and a repeated type forms the required rectangle.\nFor 4×6, index columns by the six row-pairs and color exactly each indexing pair black; every black pair and its complementary white pair then occurs once, so no rectangle exists, showing why certificate-counting is reusable when counting whole configurations is unwieldy.\nIMO 1998 Problem 2 has a contestants and odd b≥3 judges, with each judge-pair agreeing on at most k contestants; model an agreement as a triple consisting of one contestant and two agreeing judges.\nThere are at most kC(b,2) triples, while a contestant receiving p passes contributes C(p,2)+C(b−p,2), minimized for p=(b−1)/2 or (b+1)/2 at (b−1)²/4, hence a(b−1)²/4≤kb(b−1)/2 and k/a≥(b−1)/(2b).\nThis worked because one incidence had both a global upper bound and local lower bounds; variations include generalized pigeonhole bounds and weighted incidences, while common pitfalls are choosing too many box-types or forgetting equality and oddness, so practice IMO 1972 Problem 1 before studying changing states.\nPost 2 follows processes and optima, requiring induction and the impossibility of an infinite strictly decreasing sequence of nonnegative integers.\nAn invariant is unchanged by every move, not merely bounded; a monovariant changes strictly in one direction through such a discrete range, and an extremal choice selects a smallest, largest, or best finite object so that any improving modification contradicts optimality.\nIMO 1986 Problem 3 repeatedly replaces consecutive pentagon labels (x,y,z) by (x+y,−y,z+y) when y<0; the observation is that the positive total S stays fixed while squared differences may measure disorder.\nIndeed F=Σᵢ(xᵢ−xᵢ₊₂)² is a nonnegative integer, and expansion after a move gives Fnew−Fold=2Sy<0, so infinitely many moves would force an impossible infinite descent and the procedure necessarily terminates.\nThis worked because strict discrete descent converts local moves into global termination; reuse it for algorithms and games, remembering that a bounded or merely nonincreasing quantity may remain constant forever.\nIMO 1976 Problem 4 asks for the largest product of positive integers summing to 1976; the extremal observation is that every locally inferior part-pattern must be absent from a maximizing partition.\nMerge any 1 into another part, split r≥5 into 2 and r−2, replace 4 by 2+2, and replace 2+2+2 by 3+3; thus only 2s and 3s remain, at most two 2s occur, and 1976=3·658+2 forces the maximum 2·3⁶⁵⁸.\nThis worked because optimality legitimized local replacement; variants use parity or modular invariants and lexicographic monovariants, while pitfalls include failing to prove strict improvement or overlooking equality cases, so practice the 1989 IMO Shortlist chessboard invariant before replacing processes by graphs.\nPost 3 turns relationships into geometry-free pictures, requiring only finite sets and the preceding counting tools.\nA graph consists of vertices joined by edges, degree counts edges at a vertex, a path follows consecutive edges, a cycle is a closed path, and a connected component is a maximal path-connected part; a proper coloring gives adjacent vertices different colors, and this graph model encodes rather than literally equals the original situation.\nA tournament orients every edge between each vertex-pair, a directed cycle follows the arrows, indegree and outdegree count entering and leaving arrows, a regular tournament gives every vertex equal outdegree, and an Euler circuit is a closed route using every edge exactly once.\nCMO 2006 Problem 4 asks for the extreme numbers of directed three-team cycles among 2n+1 teams; the observation is to count the complementary noncyclic triples through each team’s win count wᵢ.\nA total ranking gives minimum zero, while Σwᵢ=C(2n+1,2)=n(2n+1) and every noncycle has a unique team beating the other two, so their number is ΣC(wᵢ,2), minimized when all wᵢ=n because transferring one win from counts differing by at least two decreases this sum.\nNumber teams cyclically and let each beat the next n teams to attain those equal counts, giving maximum C(2n+1,3)−(2n+1)C(n,2)=n(n+1)(2n+1)/6; reuse degree sequences and complementary counting when local orientations control global subgraphs.\nIMO Shortlist 2002 C6 asks for a cyclic permutation of 1,…,n, with even n, whose successor of x lies in {2x,2x−1,2x−n,2x−n−1}; the observation is to make numbers edge-labels rather than vertices.\nWrite n=2m, use vertices 1,…,m, and make labels 2i−1 and 2i leave i while labels i and i+m enter i, so every vertex has two entering and two leaving edges.\nFor k>1, the edge labeled k runs from ⌈k/2⌉<k to k, so induction connects vertex 1 to every vertex; repeatedly follow unused outgoing edges and splice any resulting closed tours, obtaining an Euler circuit, whose consecutive labels x,y meet at some i and therefore satisfy the four allowed formulas.\nThis worked because a required cyclic ordering became an edge-covering circuit; directed and multicolored graphs are important variants, while common pitfalls are proving degree balance without connectivity or failing to translate the circuit back, and later tools include bijections, recurrences, generating functions, probabilistic existence, and Hall’s theorem.", "raw": "{\"verdict\":\"repair\",\"missing\":[{\"requirement\":\"Add an enumerative-combinatorics post that defines and connects bijections, recurrences, and inclusion–exclusion, with a fully worked olympiad problem and guidance on choosing among these tools.\",\"draft_gap\":\"The draft covers forcing, processes, extremal arguments, and graphs, but only name-checks bijections, recurrences, and generating functions despite enumeration being a major olympiad branch.\",\"search_lead\":\"olympiad combinatorics bijection recurrence inclusion-exclusion problems\"},{\"requirement\":\"Add a selection/existence post centered on bipartite matching and Hall’s marriage theorem, including definitions of matching and neighborhood, the precise Hall condition, and a worked olympiad application.\",\"draft_gap\":\"Hall’s theorem appears only in the closing list; the series never develops the standard mechanism for proving that simultaneous choices or assignments exist.\",\"search_lead\":\"IMO Shortlist Hall marriage theorem matching combinatorics problem\"},{\"requirement\":\"Give at least one substantially different solution or viewpoint for a flagship example and compare why each method works and when it generalizes.\",\"draft_gap\":\"Every example is presented through a single intended method, so readers never see how to choose between competing combinatorial models or how the same problem can connect branches.\",\"search_lead\":\"\"}]}", "normalized": {"verdict": "repair", "missing": [{"requirement": "Add an enumerative-combinatorics post that defines and connects bijections, recurrences, and inclusion–exclusion, with a fully worked olympiad problem and guidance on choosing among these tools.", "draft_gap": "The draft covers forcing, processes, extremal arguments, and graphs, but only name-checks bijections, recurrences, and generating functions despite enumeration being a major olympiad branch.", "search_lead": "olympiad combinatorics bijection recurrence inclusion-exclusion problems"}, {"requirement": "Add a selection/existence post centered on bipartite matching and Hall’s marriage theorem, including definitions of matching and neighborhood, the precise Hall condition, and a worked olympiad application.", "draft_gap": "Hall’s theorem appears only in the closing list; the series never develops the standard mechanism for proving that simultaneous choices or assignments exist.", "search_lead": "IMO Shortlist Hall marriage theorem matching combinatorics problem"}, {"requirement": "Give at least one substantially different solution or viewpoint for a flagship example and compare why each method works and when it generalizes.", "draft_gap": "Every example is presented through a single intended method, so readers never see how to choose between competing combinatorial models or how the same problem can connect branches.", "search_lead": ""}]}, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2018, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0, "output_tokens": 1403}}
